\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Periods 1 and 2}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{The identification in \ref{fdip-00NZ} uses #{N\ge 3}. For
#{N=1} the point #{x+\varepsilon_i} equals #{x}, so the free
calculus of \ref{fdip-00NU} has no edge. Its prolongation is
concentrated in degree #{0}. The cubical complex still has one
loop in each direction. The forward difference around that loop is
zero, but the loop itself is not a form of the calculus.}

\p{For #{N=2} the step #{x+2\varepsilon_i} returns to #{x}. The
double-step relation of \ref{fdip-00NZ} is not available. The word
##{e_i(x)\,e_i(x+\varepsilon_i)
=e_{x,\,x+\varepsilon_i}\,e_{x+\varepsilon_i,\,x}}
is a backtracking word. When #{d=1} the graph is the symmetric pair
of \ref{fdip-00NY}, so the word is nonzero and the prolongation is
strictly larger than the cubical cochains. Distinct directions still
anticommute, because #{x+\varepsilon_i+\varepsilon_j} is neither
#{x} nor a neighbor when #{i\neq j}.}
